what_happened:
On September 18, 2026, the president of the United States, Donald Trump, announced on Truth Social and during an appearance in the Oval Office, the president's office in the White House, that CNN, MS NOW and Politico would be banned from the White House. He cited accumulated coverage that he called "FICTION and LIES."{c:1}{c:3}{c:90}{c:91}{c:92}{c:106}{c:107}

The same day, the White House Correspondents' Association (WHCA), the organization of reporters who cover the president of the United States, then led by Jacqui Heinrich, said the Constitution's press protections "do not depend on whether the president likes a news organization's coverage."{c:1}{c:3}{c:90}{c:91}{c:92}{c:106}{c:107}

On September 19, 2026, the Secret Service, the United States federal agency responsible for protecting the president, turned away CNN's Betsy Klein, MS NOW's Akayla Gardner and Politico's Cheyenne Haslett at the White House gates. One of the reporters said she was told "they had to take my badge because it was disabled."{c:1}{c:3}{c:90}{c:91}{c:92}{c:106}{c:107}

On September 20, 2026, the other television networks in the White House press pool, the small rotating group of reporters who cover the president in spaces too small for the full press corps and share what they gather with other outlets, declined to fill CNN's rotating slot in solidarity, according to the Associated Press. News outlets then published explainers on how the White House press pool works.{c:1}{c:3}{c:90}{c:91}{c:92}{c:106}{c:107}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect. Challenged in Cable News Network, Inc. v. Trump, No. 1:26-cv-03287, in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, the federal trial court in Washington, D.C. (filed 2026-09-21; pending; hearing scheduled for 2026-09-23).{c:107}

Beginning on October 15, 2025, reporters from at least 30 news organizations began vacating the press room at the Pentagon, the headquarters of the United States Department of Defense, removing equipment and furniture rather than sign the credential pledge the department issued on September 21, 2025. The deadline to remove belongings was October 17, 2025.{c:5}{c:93}{c:94}

The outlets that declined included ABC News, NBC News, CBS News, Fox News, CNN, the Associated Press, Reuters, Bloomberg News, The New York Times, The Washington Post, The Wall Street Journal, NPR and Politico. One America News Network was reported as the only major outlet to accept the terms.{c:5}{c:93}{c:94}

Military Times and Defense News, two publications that cover the United States armed forces and were among the outlets that declined, said the policy "threatens to punish reporters who ask legitimate questions in the course of their daily work."{c:5}{c:93}{c:94}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Partly in effect. The New York Times sued the Department of Defense on December 4, 2025. On March 20, 2026, United States District Judge Paul Friedman ruled that the credential-revocation provisions violated constitutional protections and ordered credentials restored for seven Times journalists. After the department issued revised restrictions, Friedman wrote on April 9, 2026 that it "simply cannot reinstate an unlawful policy under the guise of taking 'new' action," and the department appealed. Most other outlets cover the Pentagon from outside the building.{c:94}

On February 25, 2025, the White House announced that it would select the journalists who join the limited-space press pool that covers the president of the United States in the Oval Office, on Air Force One and at other small venues. The White House Correspondents' Association (WHCA), the organization of reporters who cover the president, and its member outlets had managed the rotation for more than a century.{c:6}{c:7}{c:95}

The WHCA president, Eugene Daniels, said the association had not been consulted and that the change "suggests the government will choose the journalists who cover the president." The WHCA said it would continue to press for comprehensive access and full transparency.{c:6}{c:7}{c:95}

The announcement came nine days after the White House began excluding the Associated Press from the Oval Office and Air Force One (see the February 2025 entry).{c:6}{c:7}{c:95}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect. The White House "now picks and chooses which members of the media make up the pool," according to the Associated Press.{c:95}

On September 17, 2025, the chairman of the Federal Communications Commission (FCC), the United States regulator that licenses radio and television stations, Brendan Carr, spoke publicly about broadcasters that carried Jimmy Kimmel Live! Kimmel had spoken on air about speculation by some of Donald Trump's supporters over the motives of the person who killed Charlie Kirk, according to NPR.{c:8}{c:96}{c:97}{c:98}

Carr said, "We can do this the easy way or the hard way. These companies can find ways to change conduct to take action on Kimmel or, you know, there's going to be additional work for the FCC ahead." NPR reported that the show was pulled "hours after the U.S. government threatened to cancel broadcasting licenses."{c:8}{c:96}{c:97}{c:98}

Nexstar and Sinclair, companies that own many local television stations affiliated with ABC, announced they would pull the show indefinitely. ABC, the network that airs it, then did the same, according to NPR.{c:8}{c:96}{c:97}{c:98}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Ended. ABC announced that Jimmy Kimmel Live! would return on September 23, 2025, according to Al Jazeera.{c:98}

Donald Trump sued CBS News' parent company, Paramount, over 60 Minutes' editing of a 2024 interview with Kamala Harris, then vice-president of the United States. The lawsuit alleged that the network aired two differently edited excerpts of the same answer, on Face the Nation and on 60 Minutes, in a deceptive way. Ronny Jackson was a co-plaintiff.{c:9}{c:99}{c:100}{c:101}

On July 1, 2025, Paramount announced a US$16 million settlement, reached with a mediator. The money goes to Trump's future presidential library and the plaintiffs' fees and costs rather than to Trump or Jackson personally. Paramount said the lawsuit was without merit and that the editing followed standard practice.{c:9}{c:99}{c:100}{c:101}

Under the settlement, 60 Minutes will release transcripts of interviews with presidential candidates, subject to redactions for legal or national security reasons.{c:9}{c:99}{c:100}{c:101}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Settled. The lawsuit ended with the settlement announced on July 1, 2025.{c:101}

In March 2025, the president of the United States, Donald Trump, signed an executive order directing federal agencies to reduce their workforce. Kari Lake, installed as a senior advisor at the U.S. Agency for Global Media (USAGM), the federal agency that oversees Voice of America (VOA), moved to shut down VOA's operations. The agency cancelled leases and placed nearly all VOA staff on administrative leave beginning on March 15, 2025.{c:40}{c:41}{c:42}{c:43}

On March 21, 2025, VOA and USAGM journalists, represented in part by the American Federation of Government Employees (AFGE), a union of United States federal employees, sued the Trump administration and Lake. They argued that the shutdown violated the law that establishes VOA's editorial independence.{c:40}{c:41}{c:42}{c:43}

On March 28, 2025, a federal judge issued a temporary restraining order requiring the agency to restore operations, in Widakuswara v. Lake. Further staff cuts and litigation continued into 2026, including a March 2026 order returning about 1,000 VOA staff to work.{c:40}{c:41}{c:42}{c:43}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Partly in effect. Litigation over VOA's staffing and operations continued into 2026, with courts repeatedly ordering the agency to restore staff and functions.{c:43}

On April 25, 2025, the Attorney General of the United States, Pam Bondi, issued a memo rescinding Department of Justice rules, adopted in a 2021 memo and a 2022 rewrite of the department's media guidelines under Attorney General Merrick Garland, that set a firm rule against subpoenaing journalists' records or compelling their testimony to identify the sources of unauthorized leaks. A subpoena is a legal order that compels a person to testify or hand over records.{c:10}{c:11}{c:102}{c:103}

The rescission returned the department to a pre-2022 balancing test that weighs law enforcement interests against press freedom. The earlier rules had been adopted after it became known that the Department of Justice, during Trump's first term as president and later under President Joe Biden, had secretly obtained reporters' phone and email records.{c:10}{c:11}{c:102}{c:103}

The Reporters Committee for Freedom of the Press, a United States non-profit that provides legal help to journalists, said leak investigations "chill newsgathering and reporting in the public interest."{c:10}{c:11}{c:102}{c:103}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect. The sources cited report no restoration of the 2021 and 2022 rules.{c:103}

On November 7, 2018, after an exchange between CNN correspondent Jim Acosta and the president of the United States, Donald Trump, at a news conference following the midterm elections, the White House revoked Acosta's hard pass, the credential that gave him routine access to the White House grounds.{c:44}{c:45}{c:46}{c:47}

CNN sued. On November 16, 2018, Judge Timothy J. Kelly of the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, the federal trial court in Washington, D.C., granted a temporary restraining order, a short-term order made before a full hearing, requiring that the pass be reinstated. He applied the due process framework of Sherrill v. Knight, a 1977 appeals court decision that a White House press pass may not be denied without notice, a chance to respond and written reasons.{c:44}{c:45}{c:46}{c:47}

The White House restored Acosta's pass on November 19, 2018, and CNN dropped its suit. The White House then issued written rules for reporters' conduct when asking questions at news conferences.{c:44}{c:45}{c:46}{c:47}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Ended. The pass was restored on November 19, 2018, and CNN dropped its lawsuit, Cable News Network, Inc. v. Trump, No. 1:18-cv-02610 (D.D.C.).{c:45}

On May 28, 2024, the governor of Louisiana, Jeff Landry, signed a law creating a 25-foot (7.6-metre) zone around working law enforcement officers. Once an officer gives a verbal warning, anyone who stays within that distance commits a crime and can face jail.{c:49}{c:50}{c:51}{c:110}

Nieman Lab, a publication about journalism, reported in August 2024 that the law limits the ability of journalists and bystanders to film police activity, including alleged misconduct, because 25 feet is farther than the distance from which most phone and body-worn video of police encounters is recorded. The Reporters Committee for Freedom of the Press, a United States non-profit that provides legal help to journalists, added Louisiana guidance to its guide to recording laws.{c:49}{c:50}{c:51}{c:110}

Journalists and civil rights observers challenged the law in federal court.{c:49}{c:50}{c:51}{c:110}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Suspended by court order. A federal judge's preliminary injunction of January 31, 2025, an order that keeps the situation as it is while a case is decided, blocks enforcement (see the January 2025 entry). Louisiana's appeal was reported pending as of mid-2026.{c:51}

Florida's "Halo Law," signed by the governor of Florida, Ron DeSantis, in 2024, took effect on January 1, 2025. Like Louisiana's buffer zone law of the same year, it sets a minimum distance that the public, including journalists, must keep from first responders performing official duties once warned to step back, and treats a violation as a crime.{c:55}{c:56}{c:57}{c:58}

The law firm Phelps Dunbar grouped Florida's law with Louisiana's as two state laws raising First Amendment concerns for reporters covering police. It noted that both leave anyone recording police to judge a safe distance on the spot or risk arrest.{c:55}{c:56}{c:57}{c:58}

Unlike Louisiana's law, Florida's had not been blocked by a court as of the sources cited.{c:55}{c:56}{c:57}{c:58}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect. The sources cited report no court order blocking it.{c:58}

Since the war in Gaza began in October 2023, the government of Israel has barred foreign journalists from entering the Gaza Strip independently. Foreign correspondents have been able to enter only on trips escorted by the Israeli military, which limits their ability to check conditions on the ground for themselves.{c:105}{c:112}{c:113}

On January 7, 2026, Al Jazeera reported a statement by the Foreign Press Association (FPA), which represents journalists from international news organizations working in Israel and Gaza. The FPA said, "Instead of presenting a plan for allowing journalists into Gaza independently and letting us work alongside our brave Palestinian colleagues, the government has decided once again to lock us out."{c:105}{c:112}{c:113}

On February 25, 2026, the Committee to Protect Journalists (CPJ), a non-profit organization in New York that documents cases of journalists imprisoned or killed worldwide, published a special report recording 129 media workers killed in 2025 and holding Israel responsible for about two of every three of those deaths. CPJ separately documented what it called a pattern of harm to Palestinian journalists and media workers, and in 2026 published a review of the method it uses to count them.{c:105}{c:112}{c:113}

Status as of 2026-02-25: In effect, according to the Committee to Protect Journalists.{c:105}

On February 16, 2025, the White House began excluding Associated Press journalists from the Oval Office, the president's office, and from Air Force One, the president's aircraft. AP's style guide had continued to use the name "Gulf of Mexico" after an executive order, a written directive of the president of the United States, Donald Trump, renamed it the "Gulf of America" for United States federal purposes. AP said it would keep using the historical name while noting the new one.{c:29}{c:30}{c:31}{c:108}

On February 21, 2025, AP sued White House officials, including Taylor Budowich, the White House deputy chief of staff, in the United States District Court for the District of Columbia, the federal trial court in Washington, D.C. (Associated Press v. Budowich, No. 1:25-cv-00532). The complaint alleges "First Amendment retaliation," a claim under the part of the United States Constitution that protects freedom of the press.{c:29}{c:30}{c:31}{c:108}

On February 25, 2025, a federal judge declined to order AP's access restored immediately while the case proceeded, according to Euronews. The case later produced a preliminary injunction, a partial stay by the D.C. Circuit, the federal appeals court for Washington, D.C., and a refusal by the full appeals court to rehear the case (see the case page).{c:29}{c:30}{c:31}{c:108}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Partly in effect. Under a partial stay granted 2 to 1 by the D.C. Circuit on 2025-06-06, AP remains excluded from the Oval Office, Air Force One, Mar-a-Lago and the Cabinet Room and keeps access to the East Room. See Associated Press v. Budowich.{c:31}

On September 21, 2025, the Department of Defense of the United States, under the Secretary of Defense, Pete Hegseth, issued press credential rules that require accredited reporters to sign a pledge not to solicit or publish unclassified but sensitive information without authorization from an appropriate Pentagon official. The rules also restrict reporters' movement inside the Pentagon, the department's headquarters, without an escort.{c:33}{c:34}{c:35}{c:109}

Hegseth said, "The press is no longer allowed to roam the halls of a secure facility. Wear a badge and follow the rules," and added, "or go home."{c:33}{c:34}{c:35}{c:109}

Military Times and Defense News, two publications that cover the United States armed forces, said the policy "threatens to punish reporters who ask legitimate questions in the course of their daily work." Most reporters with Pentagon credentials declined to sign and turned in their badges in October 2025 (see the October 2025 entry).{c:33}{c:34}{c:35}{c:109}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Partly in effect. On March 20, 2026, United States District Judge Paul Friedman ruled that the rules' credential-revocation provisions violated constitutional protections and ordered credentials restored for seven New York Times journalists. The department issued revised restrictions on March 23, 2026, which the Pentagon Press Association called "a clear violation of the letter and spirit" of the ruling. On April 9, 2026, Friedman wrote that the department "simply cannot reinstate an unlawful policy under the guise of taking 'new' action," and the department appealed. Most major outlets work without Pentagon building credentials.{c:35}

On May 1, 2025, the president of the United States, Donald Trump, signed Executive Order 14290, titled "Ending Taxpayer Subsidization of Biased Media." An executive order is a written directive through which the president manages the federal government. The order directs the Corporation for Public Broadcasting (CPB) and federal agencies to end direct and indirect funding of NPR and PBS. It says the two networks violate the requirement of balanced coverage in the Public Broadcasting Act of 1967, the United States law that created CPB.{c:36}{c:37}{c:38}{c:39}

NPR and PBS said CPB is a private, congressionally chartered non-profit and that decisions on federal funding rest with Congress, the national legislature, not with the president. Both said they would contest the order rather than comply voluntarily.{c:36}{c:37}{c:38}{c:39}

Later in 2025, Congress took separate action on public broadcasting funds, including moves to take back money it had already appropriated to CPB.{c:36}{c:37}{c:38}{c:39}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect. NPR and PBS say the order is not self-executing and have contested it; disputes over CPB funding continued through 2025 through separate action by Congress.{c:39}

On January 31, 2025, Judge John W. deGravelles of the United States District Court for the Middle District of Louisiana blocked enforcement of the state's 25-foot police buffer zone law. He ruled that the plaintiffs, including journalists and civil rights observers, were likely to succeed on their claim that the distance requirement would impede newsgathering that does not obstruct police, in breach of the First Amendment, the part of the United States Constitution that protects freedom of speech and of the press.{c:52}{c:53}{c:54}

The challenge argued that the law made routine filming of police from a normal observing distance a crime and gave officers broad discretion to order bystanders back whether or not they were interfering. Louisiana defended the law as an officer-safety measure and was reported to be appealing the injunction to the Fifth Circuit, the federal appeals court based in New Orleans.{c:52}{c:53}{c:54}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Suspended by court order. The injunction remains in place while the case is pending; Louisiana's appeal was reported pending as of mid-2026.{c:54}

On September 9, 1971, the office of Charles Colson, special counsel to the president of the United States, Richard Nixon, sent White House Counsel John Dean a memorandum, drafted by the aide George T. Bell, naming 20 people as an initial "enemies list." A longer master list of hundreds of names was compiled over the following years.{c:59}{c:60}{c:61}{c:62}

On August 16, 1971, Dean had written a memo about "how we can use the available federal machinery to screw our political enemies," including through tax audits, denial of federal grants and contracts, and litigation.{c:59}{c:60}{c:61}{c:62}

Journalists were among the people named on the versions of the list, including the CBS News correspondent Daniel Schorr. The IRS commissioner, Donald C. Alexander, refused to launch audits of the people on the list. The lists became public during the Watergate investigations.{c:59}{c:60}{c:61}{c:62}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Ended. The lists became public during the Watergate investigations and are part of the historical record.{c:62}

On October 10, 2013, the Committee to Protect Journalists published a special report documenting that, since 2009, the Department of Justice of the United States, under the president, Barack Obama, had brought felony charges under the Espionage Act against six government employees and two contractors, including Edward Snowden, for leaking classified information to journalists. Across all earlier United States administrations there had been three such prosecutions.{c:63}{c:64}{c:65}{c:66}

The report said the prosecutions, together with the collection of reporters' phone and email records in leak investigations, left sources reluctant to speak to journalists about diplomacy, national security and intelligence. One source told the report's author, "I worry now about calling somebody because the contact can be found out through a check of phone records or e-mails."{c:63}{c:64}{c:65}{c:66}

The Freedom of the Press Foundation, a United States non-profit that builds secure communication tools for journalists, later said the pattern set a precedent that later administrations, including Donald Trump's first, could extend.{c:63}{c:64}{c:65}{c:66}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Ended. The prosecutions are cited by press freedom organizations, including the Freedom of the Press Foundation, as a precedent for later administrations' use of the Espionage Act against sources.{c:66}

On December 20, 2023, the president of Hungary, Katalin Novák, promulgated the National Sovereignty Protection Act, which the National Assembly, Hungary's parliament, had passed. The law created a Sovereignty Protection Office with power to investigate organizations, including news outlets, that it determined might be influenced by foreign actors or threaten national sovereignty.{c:67}{c:68}{c:69}{c:70}

In a joint evaluation a week before promulgation, ten Hungarian media outlets said the law "is capable of severely restricting the freedom of the press, potentially making it difficult or even impossible for independent newsrooms, journalists and media companies to operate," according to Reporters Without Borders (RSF), an international organization based in Paris.{c:67}{c:68}{c:69}{c:70}

After a change of government in 2026, the National Assembly abolished the Sovereignty Protection Office on June 30, 2026. The Committee to Protect Journalists (CPJ), a non-profit organization in New York, wrote in July 2026 that the office "had been used to harass and delegitimize independent media by labeling them threats to national sovereignty," and described Hungary's initial media reforms as promising but not yet backed by lasting safeguards.{c:67}{c:68}{c:69}{c:70}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Rescinded. The National Assembly abolished the Sovereignty Protection Office on June 30, 2026. CPJ wrote that lasting safeguards for media independence are still needed.{c:70}

On February 10, 2026, the investigative journalist Ravi Nair was sentenced in India to one year's imprisonment and a fine of 5,000 rupees in a criminal defamation case over social media posts and articles critical of the Adani Group, an Indian conglomerate.{c:71}{c:72}{c:73}

CIVICUS Monitor, a research project of the global civil society alliance CIVICUS, recorded the sentence alongside other incidents in the same period: the February 18, 2026 ambush and beating of the journalist Khoirom Loyalakpa in Imphal, the capital of the state of Manipur, and police summonses of Bashaarat Masood of The Indian Express and Ashiq Hussain of Hindustan Times for questioning about their coverage of law enforcement in Kashmir.{c:71}{c:72}{c:73}

CIVICUS also reported proposed information technology rules that would extend the government's system of takedown orders to individual journalists posting on social media, which it said would add to legal pressure on independent reporting.{c:71}{c:72}{c:73}

Status as of 2026-09-22: Not publicly stated. Nair was sentenced on February 10, 2026; the sources cited do not state whether he has appealed.{c:73}

On August 4, 2026, the president of Russia, Vladimir Putin, signed into law legislation passed by the State Duma, the lower house of Russia's parliament, on July 24, 2026. It applies to Russians living in exile who have been designated "foreign agents" or accused of cooperating with organizations Russia has designated "undesirable," a group that Reporters Without Borders (RSF), an international organization based in Paris, says is made up mostly of journalists.{c:74}{c:75}{c:76}{c:77}

The law allows the authorities to freeze bank accounts and suspend online banking, ban sales of real estate and vehicles, revoke driving licences, redirect income to state-controlled accounts for seizure, block access to Russia's public services portal and deny consular services, including passport renewal.{c:74}{c:75}{c:76}{c:77}

RSF said the law adds "yet another layer to an already extensive legal arsenal aimed at crushing independent journalism." RSF and Freedom House, a United States-based organization that rates political rights and civil liberties by country, both described it as part of a series of Russian legal measures since 2012 that use the "foreign agent" and "undesirable organization" designations to isolate and financially pressure independent and exiled journalists rather than prosecute them for specific reporting.{c:74}{c:75}{c:76}{c:77}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect since August 4, 2026.{c:76}

Between February and April 2026, the government of El Salvador, under the president, Nayib Bukele, froze personal assets of individual shareholders of Trípode S.A. de C.V., the parent company of the investigative outlet El Faro. CPJ reported that the government placed "preventative annotations" on a bank account and real estate belonging to two partners of the company.{c:78}{c:79}{c:80}{c:81}

In a report published on May 8, 2026, the Committee to Protect Journalists (CPJ) said the freeze "marks a dangerous escalation in the Bukele administration's long-standing effort to dismantle El Faro through administrative harassment." El Faro has operated partly from exile.{c:78}{c:79}{c:80}{c:81}

The International Consortium of Investigative Journalists (ICIJ), an international network of investigative reporters, reported that at least 40 Salvadoran journalists had fled into exile fearing imprisonment as of mid-2025. Reporters Without Borders (RSF) has described legal, physical and financial pressure on independent Salvadoran journalism that is pushing it out of the country.{c:78}{c:79}{c:80}{c:81}

Status as of 2026-05-08: In effect, according to CPJ. No reversal is reported in the sources cited.{c:80}

On December 15, 2025, the High Court of Hong Kong convicted the media owner Jimmy Lai on two counts of conspiring to collude with foreign forces under the National Security Law, a law China's central government imposed on Hong Kong in 2020, and one count of conspiracy to publish seditious material under sedition legislation dating from British colonial rule. Hong Kong is a special administrative region of China with its own courts and legal system.{c:83}{c:84}{c:85}{c:111}

Lai, founder of the newspaper Apple Daily and the magazine Next Magazine, had been detained since his arrest in 2020. Apple Daily shut down in June 2021 after its accounts were frozen and much of its top leadership was arrested, and Next Magazine also closed.{c:83}{c:84}{c:85}{c:111}

On February 8, 2026, the court sentenced Lai to 20 years in prison. Human Rights Watch called the charges "bogus," and United Nations experts expressed dismay at the conviction.{c:83}{c:84}{c:85}{c:111}

Status as of 2026-09-22: In effect. Lai is imprisoned following his sentencing on February 8, 2026.{c:85}

On March 19, 2025, Turkish authorities detained the mayor of Istanbul, Ekrem İmamoğlu, whom CPJ described as a potential challenger to the president of Türkiye, Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, in the next presidential election. Protests followed.{c:86}{c:87}{c:88}{c:89}

Between March 19 and March 28, 2025, at least 13 journalists were detained, including the reporter İsmail Saymaz, detained at his home in Istanbul on March 19, and the photojournalist Zişan Gür, detained while covering protests on March 23. From March 21 to 23, at least 14 journalists were injured after being beaten or shot with rubber bullets by Istanbul police. On March 27, Istanbul police detained the BBC correspondent Mark Lowen and deported him to London.{c:86}{c:87}{c:88}{c:89}

In a report of April 2, 2025, the Committee to Protect Journalists (CPJ), a non-profit organization in New York, said the Turkish authorities "have since arrested thousands of protesters and have moved aggressively to tamp down media coverage" of the demonstrations.{c:86}{c:87}{c:88}{c:89}

Status as of 2025-04-02: Not publicly stated. CPJ described the detentions and restrictions on coverage as continuing amid mass arrests of protesters.{c:89}
